  "rules": {
    "exact_offer_heat_form_matrix_v1": "The seller snapshots establish exact page assertions rather than hot-route performance. M&M'S SKU B07W5B43Z8 is 11.79 dollars, rated 73 percent over twelve reviews, and described as one 38-ounce party bag with real milk chocolate inside a colorful candy shell. Skittles SKU B09DQV8CK1 is 29.99 dollars, has no posted review, and is described as ninety individually wrapped fun-size packets in a reusable tub, while its one-ounce product-dimensions field is not a resolved total edible mass. Jolly Rancher SKU B078JS8FPJ is 7.96 dollars, rated 85 percent over twelve reviews, and described as thirteen ounces of twist-wrapped hard candy with sour powder centers. Charleston Chew SKU B09Q5SNTNS is 19.95 dollars, has no posted review, and its selected title and options describe six 1.87-ounce nougat bars with a chocolatey coating; seller copy says they are often enjoyed frozen. None of the four pages measures melting, softening, leakage, wrapper adhesion, food safety or palatability in a 90-degree pannier or hiking pack.",
    "chocolate_phase_and_coating_boundary_v1": "The melting-point page defines a solid-to-liquid equilibrium that depends on conditions and includes only a generic tabular cocoa-butter value. The crystal-polymorphism page says one chemical composition can crystallize into multiple structures and that polymorphism can change physical properties without chemical change. The compound-chocolate page describes cocoa with vegetable fat and sweetener, says chocolatey or compound coating can name a candy coating, and distinguishes its processing from true chocolate. These pages explain possible mechanisms; they do not identify the current fat system, crystal form, thermal history or melting range of M&M'S or Charleston Chew, and the word chocolatey alone cannot classify an exact label or predict route survival.",
    "sugar_glass_humidity_boundary_v1": "The hard-candy page describes sugar syrup cooked to make a nearly all-sugar, negligible-water, stiff and brittle confection and calls hard candy amorphous. The glass-transition page describes an amorphous material moving gradually from hard and glassy toward viscous or rubbery as temperature rises, over a material- and history-dependent range below crystalline melting. The hygroscopy page says environmental water uptake can change physical properties such as viscosity. Together they explain why a non-chocolate sweet can fail by softening, sticking or moisture uptake rather than classic melting, but they provide no exact Skittles or Jolly Rancher threshold, humidity tolerance or wrapper result.",
    "transport_cooling_exposure_boundary_v1": "The ice-pack page says reusable packs absorb heat and that the required amount varies with contents, initial temperature, insulation, ambient temperature and direct sun. One community author praises a decades-old large cooler with ice while comments report different products and failures; another thread debates whether adding salt makes ice colder or makes cold last longer and explicitly notes that the fishermen had not timed the claim. A workout anecdote reports a paper-wrapped candy becoming soggy in a pocket. These sources motivate measuring heat, humidity, containment and cooling, but cannot establish a small pannier or backpack result, endorse a salt-ice procedure, or transfer an old cooler's performance to an exact snack path.",
    "current_label_safety_route_gate_v1": "Before testing an exact snack, verify the physical name, seller, manufacturer, SKU or UPC, market, lot or date code, net quantity, complete ingredients and allergens, filling, storage instructions, seal and wrapper integrity, delivered cost and intended serving. The shelf-stable page defines sealed room-temperature storage achieved through product and packaging controls; it does not mean texture-stable at 90 degrees, safe after a broken seal, or exempt from the exact label. Record the actual pannier and backpack internal temperature and humidity profile, direct-sun exposure, duration and position. Any unresolved label, allergen, storage, filling, seal or exposure cell blocks a safety or portability verdict.",
    "matched_hot_route_portability_trial_v1": "After current-label and safety gates pass, use only small quantities from identified lots in identical leakproof secondary pouches. Match start temperature, portion or whole-unit form, bag position, route, duration and handling; log ambient and internal temperature and humidity; and repeat across the intended pannier and backpack. If testing insulation or a frozen pack, treat it as a separate factor and record added mass, condensation and reusable-pack burden. Predeclare thresholds for leakage, wrapper adhesion, shape retention, texture acceptability, consumption, package waste and clean-up. The result is local to the tested lot, route, bag, weather and duration and cannot prove a universal 90-degree brand claim or general food safety.",
    "evidence_bounded_hot_weather_snack_choice_v1": "The packet does not justify naming an M&M shell, chocolatey coating, hard candy or old cooler as inherently summer-proof. First preserve the exact seller fields and unknown labels, separate melting from glass-transition and humidity failure, verify current labels and sealed storage conditions, instrument the actual route, and run the small matched contained trial with cooling as a separate factor. Choose only the lowest total trip cost and carry-burden exact snack path that clears identity, ingredient, allergen, storage, seal, mess, texture, waste and route-exposure thresholds. Otherwise use an already proven route-and-lot control or defer carrying that sweet in those conditions. Do not issue a universal heat-resistance, health, food-safety or brand-winner verdict."
